      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Was West Dayton redlined?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. On the federal Home Owners' Loan Corporation's 1937 map of Dayton, none of the land in today's West Dayton tracts got an A or B grade. Most of the Wright-Dunbar tract and about half of the Miami Chapel tract were graded D, \"hazardous.\""
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is West Dayton a food desert?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In 2019, the U.S. Department of Agriculture counted all six tracts as low income and five of the six as low access, meaning many people lived more than half a mile from a supermarket. Today there is no full grocery store inside the six tracts. Gem City Market, a co-op grocery that opened in 2021, is about half a mile outside them."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many people live in West Dayton?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The six census tracts in this post held 49,314 people in 1970 and about 12,900 in 2020-2024, 74% fewer."
          }
        }
      ]
